WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.822
You tap your card, and the reader beeps: OK.

2
00:00:05.822 --> 00:00:07.384
Your payment is saved.

3
00:00:07.384 --> 00:00:08.874
Saved where, exactly?

4
00:00:08.874 --> 00:00:10.364
On how many machines?

5
00:00:10.633 --> 00:00:13.441
Here's the setup from the start of this lesson.

6
00:00:13.441 --> 00:00:17.203
The payments live in a database, a program that stores records.

7
00:00:17.203 --> 00:00:23.475
One machine, the leader, takes every new payment, and writes it at the next numbered position in its log.

8
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:27.877
Two followers, f1 and f2, copy that log in order.

9
00:00:27.877 --> 00:00:31.372
f1 is 20 milliseconds away, and f2 is 50.

10
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.592
A card reader sends a payment.

11
00:00:33.592 --> 00:00:36.116
The leader writes it, and sends it on.

12
00:00:36.116 --> 00:00:39.038
At some moment, it tells the card reader OK.

13
00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:39.901
Which moment?

14
00:00:39.901 --> 00:00:43.687
That choice decides what a dying leader can take with it.

15
00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:46.386
Think back to the lesson's club notebook.

16
00:00:46.386 --> 00:00:52.247
One clerk writes each payment on the next numbered line, and posts photocopies to two other towns.

17
00:00:52.247 --> 00:00:56.135
A member hands over money, and waits for the clerk to say: noted.

18
00:00:56.367 --> 00:00:58.970
She can say it the moment the ink is dry.

19
00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:02.780
Or she can wait until a town phones back: we have your line.

20
00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:05.735
Say it early, and the member leaves sooner.

21
00:01:05.735 --> 00:01:11.443
But if the office burns down that night, a line she said "noted" for may exist nowhere else.

22
00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:12.177
Map it.

23
00:01:12.177 --> 00:01:17.117
The clerk is the leader, the notebook is the log, and the towns are followers.

24
00:01:17.117 --> 00:01:20.221
Saying "noted" is called acknowledging the write.

25
00:01:20.221 --> 00:01:23.515
And the moment a change becomes final is its commit.

26
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.802
Now the lesson's simulator, with exact numbers.

27
00:01:26.802 --> 00:01:29.376
A payment arrives every 4 milliseconds.

28
00:01:29.376 --> 00:01:34.195
First rule: the leader says OK as soon as the payment is on its own disk.

29
00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:36.106
Nobody waits at all.

30
00:01:36.106 --> 00:01:39.877
In the first second, 250 card readers hear OK.

31
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:43.029
At 1,001 milliseconds, the leader dies.

32
00:01:43.029 --> 00:01:46.965
How many payments that already heard OK are now gone?

33
00:01:46.965 --> 00:01:48.153
Pause and guess.

34
00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:51.861
Four.

35
00:01:51.861 --> 00:01:54.627
f1 had everything up to 246.

36
00:01:54.627 --> 00:02:01.840
Entries 247 to 250 were still on the wire, and they died with the leader.

37
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.360
f1 takes over as the new leader.

38
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:09.516
Four card readers heard OK for payments that exist on no running machine.

39
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:14.274
This is asynchronous replication: the copies happen in the background.

40
00:02:14.274 --> 00:02:15.744
And four is a good day.

41
00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:20.382
A follower is usually a few milliseconds behind, but nothing limits it.

42
00:02:20.382 --> 00:02:26.201
Stuck behind a slow link, it can be minutes behind, and a crash then takes minutes of payments.

43
00:02:26.433 --> 00:02:31.712
So the obvious fix: say OK only when every follower has the payment.

44
00:02:31.712 --> 00:02:32.721
Run it again.

45
00:02:32.721 --> 00:02:33.963
Nothing is lost.

46
00:02:34.233 --> 00:02:39.979
But each payment now waits 100 milliseconds, the round trip to f2, the farthest.

47
00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:41.569
And it gets worse.

48
00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:45.692
Say f2 freezes for 40 seconds while its machine is moved.

49
00:02:45.692 --> 00:02:47.645
Every payment waits for it.

50
00:02:47.645 --> 00:02:51.045
Card payments stop, everywhere, for 40 seconds.

51
00:02:51.267 --> 00:02:55.122
The whole system now moves at the speed of its sleepiest machine.

52
00:02:55.122 --> 00:02:57.317
That's fully synchronous replication.

53
00:02:57.567 --> 00:03:01.318
Most systems choose the middle: wait for any one follower.

54
00:03:01.318 --> 00:03:05.004
Each payment waits 40 milliseconds, the round trip to f1.

55
00:03:05.233 --> 00:03:10.595
And the crash loses nothing, because every acknowledged payment is on at least two machines.

56
00:03:10.595 --> 00:03:13.567
That's usually called semi-synchronous replication.

57
00:03:13.833 --> 00:03:18.511
Side by side: no waiting, 250 OKs, four lost.

58
00:03:18.511 --> 00:03:23.293
Wait for one, 40 milliseconds, 241, none lost.

59
00:03:23.293 --> 00:03:26.931
Wait for both, 100, 226, none lost.

60
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:33.756
The waiting rules sent fewer OKs only because their last payments were still waiting when the leader died.

61
00:03:34.233 --> 00:03:35.658
Now the small print.

62
00:03:35.658 --> 00:03:37.652
What if no follower answers?

63
00:03:37.652 --> 00:03:42.352
MySQL, a popular free database, waits up to 10 seconds by default.

64
00:03:42.352 --> 00:03:45.343
Then it quietly goes back to asynchronous.

65
00:03:45.343 --> 00:03:47.408
No card reader sees an error.

66
00:03:47.667 --> 00:03:52.933
The lab shows it with a 500 millisecond timeout and both followers stalled.

67
00:03:52.933 --> 00:03:55.531
The first payment waits the full 500.

68
00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:01.055
Then semi-sync switches itself off, and every later payment gets its OK at once.

69
00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:08.795
When the leader dies, 149 acknowledged payments are lost, under a setting with "synchronous" in its name.

70
00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:15.751
PostgreSQL, another free database, does the opposite: it keeps payments waiting until a follower is back.

71
00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:17.087
Neither is wrong.

72
00:04:17.087 --> 00:04:20.124
One keeps payments flowing and drops its promise.

73
00:04:20.124 --> 00:04:23.285
The other keeps its promise and stops the payments.

74
00:04:23.733 --> 00:04:24.872
So, saved where?

75
00:04:24.872 --> 00:04:29.357
On as many machines as the leader waited for before it said OK.

76
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:33.532
And here's the rule to keep: a setting's name describes a good day.

77
00:04:33.532 --> 00:04:35.587
Find out what it does on a bad one.

78
00:04:36.367 --> 00:04:37.596
One quick check.

79
00:04:37.596 --> 00:04:41.054
Move f1 to 2 milliseconds away, and f2 to 70.

80
00:04:41.054 --> 00:04:45.511
Waiting for one follower, how long does each payment wait?

81
00:04:45.511 --> 00:04:46.740
Pause and think.

82
00:04:49.967 --> 00:04:53.189
4 milliseconds: the round trip to f1.

83
00:04:53.189 --> 00:04:55.889
Waiting for both now costs 140.
